Optimal Governance and Task Allocation in Heterogeneous Human–AI–Robot Organizations Under Accountability, Trust, and Uncertainty Constraints**

### A Theoretical Dissertation Toward the Emergence of the Human–AI Team Architect Profession

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude 

## **Abstract**

The accelerating integration of foundation models, autonomous AI agents, and robotic systems into institutional workflows is producing a structural discontinuity in organizational theory that existing management science and computer science frameworks are individually insufficient to address. This dissertation introduces and formally characterizes a new professional role — the **Human–AI Team Architect (HATA)** — defined as the disciplinary practitioner responsible for optimizing the allocation of cognition, decision authority, accountability, and execution across heterogeneous ensembles of human, artificial, and robotic agents. We develop a multi-layer theoretical framework grounded in stochastic optimization, constrained accountability theory, trust-aware routing, organizational graph dynamics, and mechanism design. Drawing on the current state of human–machine teaming research, the World Economic Forum's projection that 170 million new roles will emerge globally by 2030 while 92 million are displaced, and recent empirical findings indicating that 47% of current work tasks are performed primarily by humans, 22% primarily by technology, and 30% in hybrid collaboration, we argue that the bottleneck of AI adoption has decisively shifted from model capability to organizational architecture. The dissertation proposes eight interconnected research programs, a formal optimization framework with epistemic stratification of its claims, and a long-term institutional vision for the HATA profession as the foundational cognitive infrastructure designer of the AI era.

