## **Chapter 2 — Literature Review**

### 2.1 Human–Machine Teaming: Current State

The scientific study of task allocation in human–machine systems has a substantial tradition. Sheridan and Verplank proposed ten levels for decision-making in human–automation teaming, ranging from level 1 (no automation) to level 10 (full automation). Parasuraman, Sheridan, and Wickens subsequently developed a model incorporating automation applied to four cognitive functions — information acquisition, information analysis, decision and action selection, and action implementation — each scalable across this continuum. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9468009/) These foundational frameworks, while valuable, assume a largely binary agent landscape (human or machine) and fail to address the complexity of contemporary hybrid ensembles involving foundation model agents, task-specific robotic systems, and human experts operating at variable cognitive loads.

A systematic review of 5,467 papers on human–machine teaming published between 2000 and 2024 identified critical findings: humans and machines should complement each other's weaknesses; shared decision-making between humans and machines is necessary for genuine collaboration; trust and transparency depend on mutually understood capacity and progress; and explainable AI can build better trust between teammates. [NSF PAGES](https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10616149)

Emerging areas including AI ethics and synergistic human–AI collaboration have gained traction in recent years, driven by the increasing need for ethical considerations and user-centric design in human–machine teaming systems. Metrics such as response time, mission success rate, prediction accuracy, agent reliability, task allocation efficiency, and transparency have been established as objective measures of machine capability within teaming contexts. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.16518v1)

### 2.2 Multi-Agent Task Allocation

Recent research in multi-robot task allocation has focused on handling complex objectives and feasibility constraints. A general formulation has been developed that incorporates the states of robots, tasks, and the surrounding environment, including optimization-based and market-based solution approaches. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.09650) These purely machine-centric frameworks must be extended to the heterogeneous human–AI–robot setting, where qualitatively distinct agent properties — including legal standing, cognitive load, moral responsibility, and variable reliability — introduce constraints with no counterpart in purely robotic systems.

Adaptive task allocation in multi-human multi-robot teams under team heterogeneity and dynamic information uncertainty represents a frontier challenge, with cognitive load-based affective workload allocation emerging as a critical design consideration. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.16266)

### 2.3 Accountability and Governance in AI Organizations

Frameworks for answerability require that AI developers and operators be able to justify their actions and decisions through both transparency and oversight mechanisms, which are especially important in high-stakes domains. The justification of design choices requires understanding inherent trade-offs between criteria such as explainability and accuracy. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.19361)

Strong workflow design often functions as a form of governance. Leaders can specify which decisions require human sign-off and which need to be audited over time for error or bias. When these expectations are unclear, teams tend to shift toward overreliance or underuse when new tools are implemented. Regulatory approaches like the EU AI Act's human oversight requirements reinforce that effective oversight mechanisms must be designed into the operation of AI systems. [Stanford Graduate School of Business](https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/exec-ed/difference/how-ai-reshaping-future-work)

Data-driven supervisory systems are not merely technical tools but also carriers of organizational values and power structures. Their design and deployment must be guided by ethical commitments to inclusiveness, accountability, and human dignity. The growing integration of automated management tools calls for sustained interdisciplinary research into how employees perceive and respond to this shift in managerial authority. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14231v1)

### 2.4 Organizational Digital Twins

Agentic AI is increasingly being integrated into digital twins, enabling autonomous agents to monitor system conditions and make real-time decisions without human intervention. These agents exhibit goal-directed behaviour, perceiving their environment, reasoning through alternatives, and taking actions to optimize outcomes. [ISB](https://www.isb.edu/faculty-and-research/srini-raju-centre-for-it-and-the-networked-economy/digital-twins-a-strategic-decision-making-catalyst-for-business-leaders) The digital twin market is expected to grow from USD 24.5 billion in 2025 to USD 259.3 billion by 2032, with industries such as smart cities, aerospace, healthcare and manufacturing driving growth. [IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/digital-twin) This infrastructure provides the substrate on which organizational digital twins for human–AI team simulation can be constructed.

