## **Chapter 5 — The Accountability Layer: Legal Non-Delegability Constraints**

### 5.1 The Non-Delegation Principle

A defining structural feature of advanced organizations is that certain decisions cannot legally or ethically be delegated to non-human agents. These include, but are not limited to: medical diagnosis affecting individual treatment; judicial rulings; military targeting decisions; corporate fiduciary judgments; and custody determinations in family law contexts. Emerging multi-layered accountability frameworks converge on the necessity of technical verifiers, human supervisors, and institutional policies working in concert — no single layer is sufficient. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.24047)

We formalize this through the **non-delegation indicator**:

$$H(t) \in \{0, 1\}$$

where $H(t) = 1$ indicates that task $t$ requires either direct human execution or verified human oversight.

### 5.2 Constrained Optimization with Accountability

The accountability constraint modifies the assignment policy as follows. For any task with $H(t) = 1$:

$$\phi(t) \in \mathcal{A}_H \quad \text{OR} \quad \exists h \in \mathcal{A}_H : h \text{ provides verified oversight of } \phi(t)$$

The full constrained optimization problem is:

$$\max_{\phi} \; \mathbb{E}[U(\phi)]$$

$$\text{s.t.} \quad \sum_{t \in \phi^{-1}(a)} w(t) \leq C_a \quad \forall a \in \mathcal{A}$$

$$\phi(t) \in \mathcal{A}_H \cup \mathcal{A}_H^{\text{supervised}} \quad \forall t : H(t) = 1$$

$$\sum_{t \in \phi^{-1}(h)} w(t) \leq C_h^{\max} \quad \forall h \in \mathcal{A}_H$$

$$\mathbb{P}[\phi(t) \text{ fails}] \leq \delta_{\text{crit}} \quad \forall t : \text{critical}(t) = 1$$

where $\mathcal{A}_H^{\text{supervised}}$ denotes tasks assigned to AI agents under real-time human oversight, $C_h^{\max}$ is the cognitive capacity ceiling of human agents, and $\delta_{\text{crit}}$ is the maximum tolerable failure probability for safety-critical tasks.

### 5.3 The Accountability–Efficiency Frontier

**Proposition 1:** The set of Pareto-optimal solutions to the constrained problem forms a manifold in the space $\mathbb{R}^2$ of (expected utility, accountability coverage), and this frontier is strictly dominated by the unconstrained optimum.

**Proof sketch:** The unconstrained optimum may assign any task to any agent. Adding accountability constraints $H(t) = 1 \Rightarrow \phi(t) \in \mathcal{A}_H^{\text{supervised}}$ strictly reduces the feasible set of policies, thereby reducing or preserving (never improving) the maximum achievable expected utility. The efficiency loss $\Delta U = U^* - U^*_{\text{constrained}} \geq 0$ is the institutional price of accountability. $\square$

The HATA's core practical problem is navigating this frontier — finding policy $\phi$ that achieves accountability compliance while minimizing efficiency loss, given the legal and ethical parameters of the specific institutional context.

