## **Chapter 9 — Eight Research Programs of the HATA Dissertation Agenda**

### Research Program 1: Dynamic Task Allocation Under Non-Stationarity

Extending the static allocation framework $\phi : \mathcal{T} \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ to a dynamic setting where tasks arrive stochastically, agent capacities fluctuate, and performance probabilities $p(a,t)$ evolve in real time. The core question is whether approximate dynamic programming or real-time MARL can sustain near-optimal allocation at institutional scale.

### Research Program 2: Human-in-the-Loop Optimization with Endogenous Oversight Timing

Formalizing the optimal timing and depth of human oversight as a decision variable rather than a fixed parameter. Variable autonomy — the ability of robotic systems to dynamically vary their level or degree of autonomy based on context — provides a conceptual foundation that this program extends to AI agents and institutional decision flows. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11576532/) The oversight timing problem is a stochastic optimal stopping problem with accountability constraints.

### Research Program 3: Trust Calibration and Trust-Aware Agent Routing

Developing empirical protocols for calibrating $p(a,t)$ and $\tau_a$ from historical organizational performance data, and deriving routing policies $\phi_\tau$ that weight assignment decisions by trust in a formally justified manner. Key open problem: when trust and efficiency conflict, what is the normatively correct trade-off?

### Research Program 4: Accountability-Constrained Multi-Objective Planning

Formalizing the Pareto frontier between organizational efficiency and accountability coverage, and developing algorithms that efficiently traverse this frontier to support institutional policy choices. Application domains include hospital AI deployment, judicial assistance systems, and autonomous vehicle fleet management.

### Research Program 5: Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Heterogeneous Organizations

Designing MARL algorithms specifically suited to the partially adversarial, legally constrained, accountability-sensitive setting of human–AI–robot organizations — a setting not addressed by existing cooperative MARL literature.

### Research Program 6: Mechanism Design for Hybrid Workforce Incentive Compatibility

Deriving analytic solutions to Q1–Q4 in Section 7.3 under progressively relaxed assumptions, and developing computational mechanism design algorithms for settings where analytic tractability fails.

### Research Program 7: Organizational Digital Twin Methodology

Using digital twins of work to simulate and optimize workflows represents an emerging imperative for organizations undergoing AI-driven workforce transformation. [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org/stories/2026/02/workforce-transformation-ai-jobs/) This program develops formal standards for digital twin fidelity, calibration protocols, and uncertainty quantification within organizational simulation environments.

### Research Program 8: AI-Assisted Institutional Design

Developing systems in which AI agents assist the HATA in designing other AI agent systems — a recursive institutional design challenge that raises deep questions about meta-alignment and the limits of self-referential organizational planning.

