## **Chapter 10 — Epistemological Stratification of Claims**

In the spirit of formal intellectual honesty, we stratify the claims of this dissertation by their epistemic status:

**Level A — Formal Results (Proven):**
- Existence of optimal allocation policy under regularity conditions (Claim 1)
- NP-hardness of the unconstrained allocation problem (Claim 2)
- Non-negativity of the efficiency cost of accountability constraints (Proposition 1)

**Level B — Well-Supported Theoretical Conjectures:**

- Trust propagation convergence under connected graph and positive direct experience weighting (Claim 3) — requires proof of fixed-point existence under the specific functional forms proposed
- Distributional robustness of minimax formulation — relies on convexity assumptions that may not hold in all organizational settings

**Level C — Research Hypotheses (Empirically Unverified):**
- That MARL policy optimization converges to near-optimal solutions in the constrained organizational setting
- That the digital twin approximation error $\epsilon_{\text{twin}}$ can be maintained below a threshold sufficient for reliable policy testing
- That the HATA profession will emerge at institutional scale by 2035–2040

**Level D — Normative Propositions (Contested):**
- The appropriate weighting of efficiency versus accountability in the constrained optimization
- The correct mechanism for compensating human agents for supervisory labor in hybrid organizations
- The appropriate scope of legal non-delegability in AI-assisted decision systems

